  <section class="section scheme-light" aria-labelledby="what-title">
    <div class="container split-rows">
      <div class="ai-def">
        <h2 class="h2" id="what-title" data-split>What does a UI/UX design studio do?</h2>
        <div class="text-lg prose" data-reveal>
          <p>A UI/UX design studio designs how a digital product works and how it looks. UX is the structure: research, user flows, information architecture and the decisions behind each screen. UI is the surface: layout, type, colour, components and motion. A good studio does both in one loop, so the look never fights the logic.</p>
          <p>Libre Studio is a remote product design agency for startups. We design products end to end, on <a class="text-link" href="/design-systems/">atomic design foundations</a>, so the app, the marketing site and the motion all grow from one system.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="section-title narrow">
          <h2 class="h2" id="design-title" data-split>What we design</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="tools" data-reveal-group>
          <div class="tools__group" data-reveal>
            <p class="eyebrow">Apps</p>
            <dl>
              <dt>Mobile apps</dt><dd>iOS and Android products, from the first screen to the last state.</dd>
              <dt>Web apps</dt><dd>Signed-in products that people use every day.</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="tools__group" data-reveal>
            <p class="eyebrow">Data</p>
            <dl>
              <dt>SaaS dashboards</dt><dd>Stat cards, charts, tables and navigation that stay readable as data grows.</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="tools__group" data-reveal>
            <p class="eyebrow">Web</p>
            <dl>
              <dt>Websites</dt><dd>Marketing sites and landing pages built on the same tokens as the product.</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <div class="tools__group" data-reveal>
            <p class="eyebrow">Moments that decide</p>
            <dl>
              <dt>Onboarding</dt><dd>Setup that shows value before it asks for anything.</dd>
              <dt>Paywalls</dt><dd>Offers that arrive at the right moment, in plain words.</dd>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section scheme-coral" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-title center narrow">
        <h2 class="h2" id="how-title" data-split>How do we work, from research to prototype?</h2>
        <p class="text-lg" data-reveal>The same four steps on every project. Inside the design step, our AI cycle runs seven gates: AI turns research into sourced findings, prototypes from the specs and tokens, and runs design QA, and a designer approves every gate. <a class="text-link" href="/ai-design/#cycle">See the full cycle</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="process">
        <div class="process__line" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <ol class="process__steps" data-stagger>
          <li class="process__step"><span class="process__num" aria-hidden="true">01</span><div class="stack"><h3 class="h5">Strategy</h3><p>A kickoff call, then time, budget and milestone estimates, and a small demo task before anything is signed.</p></div></li>
          <li class="process__step"><span class="process__num" aria-hidden="true">02</span><div class="stack"><h3 class="h5">Design</h3><p>UX, UI and prototyping in one loop, with AI in the workflow and QA before every handoff.</p></div></li>
          <li class="process__step"><span class="process__num" aria-hidden="true">03</span><div class="stack"><h3 class="h5">Development</h3><p>A token-strict Figma file your developers build from, or a no-code build when that ships faster.</p></div></li>
          <li class="process__step"><span class="process__num" aria-hidden="true">04</span><div class="stack"><h3 class="h5">Measure &amp; Optimise</h3><p>A post-project review, then test, measure and optimise what is live.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

